## sn45.34
sn45.34:1.1 | S: At Sāvatthī. | T: At Savatthī.
sn45.34:1.2 | S: “Mendicants, when these eight things are developed and cultivated they lead to going from the near shore to the far shore. | T: “Bhikkhus, these eight things, when developed and cultivated, lead to going beyond from the near shore to the far shore.
sn45.34:1.3 | S: What eight? | T: What eight?
sn45.34:1.4 | S: They are right view, right purpose, right speech, right action, right livelihood, right effort, right mindfulness, and right immersion. | T: Right view … right concentration.
sn45.34:1.5 | S: When these eight things are developed and cultivated they lead to going from the near shore to the far shore.” | T: These eight things, when developed and cultivated, lead to going beyond from the near shore to the far shore.”
sn45.34:2.1 | S: That is what the Buddha said. | T: This is what the Blessed One said.
sn45.34:2.2 | S: Then the Holy One, the Teacher, went on to say: | T: Having said this, the Fortunate One, the Teacher, further said this:
sn45.34:3.1 | S: “Few are those among humans | T: “Few are those among humankind
sn45.34:3.2 | S: who cross to the far shore. | T: Who go beyond to the far shore.
sn45.34:3.3 | S: The rest just run | T: The rest of the people merely run
sn45.34:3.4 | S: around on the near shore. | T: Up and down along the bank.
sn45.34:4.1 | S: When the teaching is well explained, | T: “When the Dhamma is rightly expounded
sn45.34:4.2 | S: the people who practice accordingly | T: Those who practise in accord with the Dhamma
sn45.34:4.3 | S: will cross over | T: Are the people who will go beyond
sn45.34:4.4 | S: Death’s dominion so hard to pass. | T: The realm of Death so hard to cross.
sn45.34:5.1 | S: Rid of dark qualities, | T: “Having left behind the dark qualities,
sn45.34:5.2 | S: an astute person would develop the bright. | T: The wise man should develop the bright ones.
sn45.34:5.3 | S: Having left home for homelessness | T: Having come from home into homelessness,
sn45.34:5.4 | S: in seclusion, where joy is hard, | T: Where it is hard to take delight—
sn45.34:6.1 | S: they’d long for satisfaction there. | T: “There in seclusion he should seek delight,
sn45.34:6.2 | S: Forsaking sensual pleasures, owning nothing, | T: Having left behind sensual pleasures. Owning nothing, the wise man
sn45.34:6.3 | S: an astute person would cleanse themselves | T: Should cleanse himself
sn45.34:6.4 | S: of mental corruptions. | T: of mental defilements.
sn45.34:7.1 | S: And those whose minds are rightly developed | T: “Those whose minds are well developed
sn45.34:7.2 | S: in the awakening factors; | T: In the factors of enlightenment,
sn45.34:7.3 | S: who, letting go of attachments, | T: Who through nonclinging find delight
sn45.34:7.4 | S: delight in not grasping— | T: In the relinquishment of grasping:
sn45.34:7.5 | S: with defilements ended, brilliant, | T: Those luminous ones with taints destroyed
sn45.34:7.6 | S: they are quenched in this world.” | T: Are fully quenched in the world.”
sn45.34:7.7 | P: Catutthaṁ. | T: (none)

