## sn45.5
sn45.5:1.1 | S: At Sāvatthī. | T: At Savatthī.
sn45.5:1.2 | S: Then several mendicants went up to the Buddha … and said to him: | T: Then a number of bhikkhus approached the Blessed One…. Sitting to one side, those bhikkhus said to the Blessed One:
sn45.5:2.1 | S: “Sir, sometimes wanderers of other religions ask us: | T: “Here, venerable sir, wanderers of other sects ask us:
sn45.5:2.2 | S: ‘Reverends, what’s the goal of leading the spiritual life under the ascetic Gotama?’ | T: ‘For what purpose, friends, is the holy life lived under the ascetic Gotama?’
sn45.5:2.3 | S: We answer them like this: | T: When we are asked thus, venerable sir, we answer those wanderers thus:
sn45.5:2.4 | S: ‘The goal of leading the spiritual life under the Buddha is to completely understand suffering.’ | T: ‘It is, friends, for the full understanding of suffering that the holy life is lived under the Blessed One.’
sn45.5:2.5 | S: Answering this way, we trust that we repeat what the Buddha has said, and don’t misrepresent him with an untruth. We trust our explanation is in line with the teaching, and that there are no legitimate grounds for rebuttal or criticism.” | T: We hope, venerable sir, that when we answer thus we state what has been said by the Blessed One and do not misrepresent him with what is contrary to fact; that we explain in accordance with the Dhamma, and that no reasonable consequence of our assertion gives ground for criticism.”
sn45.5:3.1 | S: “Indeed, in answering this way you repeat what I’ve said, and don’t misrepresent me with an untruth. Your explanation is in line with the teaching, and there are no legitimate grounds for rebuttal or criticism. | T: “Surely, bhikkhus, when you answer thus you state what has been said by me and do not misrepresent me with what is contrary to fact; you explain in accordance with the Dhamma, and no reasonable consequence of your assertion gives ground for criticism.
sn45.5:3.2 | S: For the goal of leading the spiritual life under me is to completely understand suffering. | T: For, bhikkhus, it is for the full understanding of suffering that the holy life is lived under me.
sn45.5:3.3 | S: If wanderers of other religions were to ask you: | T: “If, bhikkhus, wanderers of other sects ask you:
sn45.5:3.4 | S: ‘Is there a path and a practice for completely understanding that suffering?’ You should answer them like this: | T: ‘But, friends, is there a path, is there a way for the full understanding of this suffering? ’—being asked thus, you should answer them thus:
sn45.5:3.5 | S: ‘There is.’ | T: ‘There is a path, friends, there is a way for the full understanding of this suffering.’
sn45.5:4.1 | S: And what is that path? | T: “And what, bhikkhus, is that path, what is that way for the full understanding of this suffering?
sn45.5:4.2 | S: It is simply this noble eightfold path, that is: | T: It is this Noble Eightfold Path; that is,
sn45.5:4.3 | S: right view, right purpose, right speech, right action, right livelihood, right effort, right mindfulness, and right immersion. | T: right view … right concentration.
sn45.5:4.4 | S: This is the path and the practice for completely understanding suffering. | T: This is the path, this is the way for the full understanding of this suffering.
sn45.5:4.5 | S: When questioned by wanderers of other religions, that’s how you should answer them.” | T: “Being asked thus, bhikkhus, you should answer those wanderers of other sects in such a way.”
sn45.5:4.6 | P: Pañcamaṁ. | T: (none)

