## sn45.84
sn45.84:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, I do not see a single thing that gives rise to the noble eightfold path, or, if it’s already arisen, fully develops it like good friendship. | T: “Bhikkhus, I do not see even one other thing by means of which the unarisen Noble Eightfold Path arises and the arisen Noble Eightfold Path goes to fulfilment by development so effectively as by this: good friendship.
sn45.84:1.2 | S: A mendicant with good friends can expect to develop and cultivate the noble eightfold path. | T: When a bhikkhu has a good friend, it is to be expected that he will develop and cultivate the Noble Eightfold Path.
sn45.84:1.3 | S: And how does a mendicant with good friends develop and cultivate the noble eightfold path? | T: “And how does a bhikkhu who has a good friend develop and cultivate the Noble Eightfold Path?
sn45.84:1.4 | S: It’s when a mendicant develops right view, right purpose, right speech, right action, right livelihood, right effort, right mindfulness, and right immersion, which culminate in the removal of greed, hate, and delusion. | T: Here, bhikkhus, a bhikkhu develops right view, which has as its final goal the removal of lust, the removal of hatred, the removal of delusion…. He develops right concentration, which has as its final goal the removal of lust, the removal of hatred, the removal of delusion.
sn45.84:1.5 | S: That’s how a mendicant with good friends develops and cultivates the noble eightfold path.” | T: It is in this way, bhikkhus, that a bhikkhu who has a good friend develops and cultivates the Noble Eightfold Path.”
sn45.84:1.6 | P: Paṭhamaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn45.8
sn45.8:1.1 | S: At Sāvatthī. | T: At Savatthī.
sn45.8:1.2 | S: “Mendicants, I will teach and analyze for you the noble eightfold path. | T: “Bhikkhus, I will teach you the Noble Eightfold Path and I will analyse it for you.
sn45.8:1.3 | S: Listen and apply your mind well, I will speak.” | T: Listen to that and attend closely, I will speak.”
sn45.8:1.4 | S: “Yes, sir,” they replied. | T: “Yes, venerable sir,” those bhikkhus replied.
sn45.8:1.5 | S: The Buddha said this: | T: The Blessed One said this:
sn45.8:2.1 | S: “And what is the noble eightfold path? | T: “And what, bhikkhus, is the Noble Eightfold Path?
sn45.8:2.2 | S: It is right view, right purpose, right speech, right action, right livelihood, right effort, right mindfulness, and right immersion. | T: Right view … right concentration.
sn45.8:3.1 | S: And what is right view? | T: “And what, bhikkhus, is right view?
sn45.8:3.2 | S: Knowing about suffering, the origin of suffering, the cessation of suffering, and the practice that leads to the cessation of suffering. | T: Knowledge of suffering, knowledge of the origin of suffering, knowledge of the cessation of suffering, knowledge of the way leading to the cessation of suffering:
sn45.8:3.3 | S: This is called right view. | T: this is called right view.
sn45.8:4.1 | S: And what is right purpose? | T: “And what, bhikkhus, is right intention?
sn45.8:4.2 | S: The purpose of renunciation, good will, and harmlessness. | T: Intention of renunciation, intention of non-ill will, intention of harmlessness:
sn45.8:4.3 | S: This is called right purpose. | T: this is called right intention.
sn45.8:5.1 | S: And what is right speech? | T: “And what, bhikkhus, is right speech?
sn45.8:5.2 | S: Refraining from speech that’s false, backbiting, harsh, or nonsensical. | T: Abstinence from false speech, abstinence from divisive speech, abstinence from harsh speech, abstinence from idle chatter:
sn45.8:5.3 | S: This is called right speech. | T: this is called right speech.
sn45.8:6.1 | S: And what is right action? | T: “And what, bhikkhus, is right action?
sn45.8:6.2 | S: Refraining from killing living creatures, stealing, and unchastity. | T: Abstinence from the destruction of life, abstinence from taking what is not given, abstinence from sexual misconduct:
sn45.8:6.3 | S: This is called right action. | T: this is called right action.
sn45.8:7.1 | S: And what is right livelihood? | T: “And what, bhikkhus, is right livelihood?
sn45.8:7.2 | S: It’s when a noble disciple gives up wrong livelihood and earns a living by right livelihood. | T: Here a noble disciple, having abandoned a wrong mode of livelihood, earns his living by a right livelihood:
sn45.8:7.3 | S: This is called right livelihood. | T: this is called right livelihood.
sn45.8:8.1 | S: And what is right effort? | T: “And what, bhikkhus, is right effort?
sn45.8:8.2 | S: It’s when a mendicant generates enthusiasm, tries, makes an effort, exerts the mind, and strives so that bad, unskillful qualities don’t arise. | T: Here, bhikkhus, a bhikkhu generates desire for the nonarising of unarisen evil unwholesome states; he makes an effort, arouses energy, applies his mind, and strives.
sn45.8:8.3 | S: They generate enthusiasm, try, make an effort, exert the mind, and strive so that bad, unskillful qualities that have arisen are given up. | T: He generates desire for the abandoning of arisen evil unwholesome states….
sn45.8:8.4 | S: They generate enthusiasm, try, make an effort, exert the mind, and strive so that skillful qualities arise. | T: He generates desire for the arising of unarisen wholesome states….
sn45.8:8.5 | S: They generate enthusiasm, try, make an effort, exert the mind, and strive so that skillful qualities that have arisen remain, are not lost, but increase, mature, and are fully developed. | T: He generates desire for the maintenance of arisen wholesome states, for their nondecay, increase, expansion, and fulfilment by development; he makes an effort, arouses energy, applies his mind, and strives.
sn45.8:8.6 | S: This is called right effort. | T: This is called right effort.
sn45.8:9.1 | S: And what is right mindfulness? | T: “And what, bhikkhus is right mindfulness?
sn45.8:9.2 | S: It’s when a mendicant meditates by observing an aspect of the body—keen, aware, and mindful, rid of covetousness and displeasure for the world. | T: Here, bhikkhus, a bhikkhu dwells contemplating the body in the body, ardent, clearly comprehending, mindful, having removed covetousness and displeasure in regard to the world.
sn45.8:9.3 | S: They meditate observing an aspect of feelings—keen, aware, and mindful, rid of covetousness and displeasure for the world. | T: He dwells contemplating feelings in feelings, ardent, clearly comprehending, mindful, having removed covetousness and displeasure in regard to the world.
sn45.8:9.4 | S: They meditate observing an aspect of the mind—keen, aware, and mindful, rid of covetousness and displeasure for the world. | T: He dwells contemplating mind in mind, ardent, clearly comprehending, mindful, having removed covetousness and displeasure in regard to the world.
sn45.8:9.5 | S: They meditate observing an aspect of principles—keen, aware, and mindful, rid of covetousness and displeasure for the world. | T: He dwells contemplating phenomena in phenomena, ardent, clearly comprehending, mindful, having removed covetousness and displeasure in regard to the world.
sn45.8:9.6 | S: This is called right mindfulness. | T: This is called right mindfulness.
sn45.8:10.1 | S: And what is right immersion? | T: “And what, bhikkhus, is right concentration?
sn45.8:10.2 | S: It’s when a mendicant, quite secluded from sensual pleasures, secluded from unskillful qualities, enters and remains in the first absorption, which has the rapture and bliss born of seclusion, while placing the mind and keeping it connected. | T: Here, bhikkhus, secluded from sensual pleasures, secluded from unwholesome states, a bhikkhu enters and dwells in the first jhana, which is accompanied by thought and examination, with rapture and happiness born of seclusion.
sn45.8:10.3 | S: As the placing of the mind and keeping it connected are stilled, they enter and remain in the second absorption, which has the rapture and bliss born of immersion, with internal clarity and mind at one, without placing the mind and keeping it connected. | T: With the subsiding of thought and examination, he enters and dwells in the second jhana, which has internal confidence and unification of mind, is without thought and examination, and has rapture and happiness born of concentration.
sn45.8:10.4 | S: And with the fading away of rapture, they enter and remain in the third absorption, where they meditate with equanimity, mindful and aware, experiencing for themselves the bliss of which the noble ones declare, ‘Equanimous and mindful, one meditates in bliss.’ | T: With the fading away as well of rapture, he dwells equanimous and, mindful and clearly comprehending, he experiences happiness with the body; he enters and dwells in the third jhana of which the noble ones declare: ‘He is equanimous, mindful, one who dwells happily.’
sn45.8:10.5 | S: With the giving up of pleasure and pain and the disappearance of former happiness and sadness, they enter and remain in the fourth absorption, without pleasure or pain, with pure equanimity and mindfulness. | T: With the abandoning of pleasure and pain, and with the previous passing away of joy and displeasure, he enters and dwells in the fourth jhana, which is neither painful nor pleasant and includes the purification of mindfulness by equanimity.
sn45.8:10.6 | S: This is called right immersion.” | T: This is called right concentration.”
sn45.8:10.7 | P: Aṭṭhamaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn45.91
sn45.91:1.1 | S: At Sāvatthī. | T: At Savatthī.
sn45.91:1.2 | S: “Mendicants, the Ganges River slants, slopes, and inclines to the east. | T: “Bhikkhus, just as the river Ganges slants, slopes, and inclines towards the east,
sn45.91:1.3 | S: In the same way, a mendicant who develops and cultivates the noble eightfold path slants, slopes, and inclines to extinguishment. | T: so too a bhikkhu who develops and cultivates the Noble Eightfold Path slants, slopes, and inclines towards Nibbāna.
sn45.91:1.4 | S: And how does a mendicant who develops the noble eightfold path slant, slope, and incline to extinguishment? | T: “And how, bhikkhus, does a bhikkhu develop and cultivate the Noble Eightfold Path so that he slants, slopes, and inclines towards Nibbāna?
sn45.91:1.5 | S: It’s when a mendicant develops right view, right purpose, right speech, right action, right livelihood, right effort, right mindfulness, and right immersion, which rely on seclusion, fading away, and cessation, and ripen as letting go. | T: Here, bhikkhus, a bhikkhu develops right view, which is based upon seclusion, dispassion, and cessation, maturing in release…. He develops right concentration, which is based upon seclusion, dispassion, and cessation, maturing in release.
sn45.91:1.6 | S: That’s how a mendicant who develops and cultivates the noble eightfold path slants, slopes, and inclines to extinguishment.” | T: It is in this way, bhikkhus, that a bhikkhu develops and cultivates the Noble Eightfold Path so that he slants, slopes, and inclines towards Nibbāna.”
sn45.91:1.7 | P: Paṭhamaṁ. | T: (none)

